00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.500
And today we're going to be diving
into the world of organic chemistry.

00:00:07.730 --> 00:00:13.010
Specifically, we're going to be looking
at how to name alkanes with branches.

00:00:13.509 --> 00:00:17.340
Now, this is part of my organic,
introductory organic chemistry series.

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:20.019
So let's really go into how
we can start naming these.

00:00:20.469 --> 00:00:27.230
So prior, alkanes and that there is
a very systematic way to doing these.

00:00:27.619 --> 00:00:31.369
Now, with branches, which are
commonly known as substituents,

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.640
are parts that are breaking off the
main chain and can be a little bit

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:37.230
tricky when it comes to naming them.

00:00:37.715 --> 00:00:40.605
The good thing is we're doing
this systematically, so it all

00:00:40.635 --> 00:00:44.714
comes down to how many carbons are
there within a given chain, right?

00:00:44.714 --> 00:00:49.404
So most of your different branches are
generally very small, typically they don't

00:00:49.405 --> 00:00:53.415
go above 5 carbons, but it is possible
for you to even get larger than this.

00:00:53.945 --> 00:00:57.885
So now when it comes to most, most of
these, right, if you think of the, of one

00:00:57.885 --> 00:01:00.525
carbon alkane, you think methane, right?

00:01:00.595 --> 00:01:02.684
Especially when you think of that alkane.

00:01:03.054 --> 00:01:07.024
So whenever we're looking at these,
so the very first species is it,

00:01:07.024 --> 00:01:10.744
you're just taking the prefix of
it, which is that meth part of it.

00:01:11.214 --> 00:01:15.104
And then you're going to be
adding the essentially the suffix

00:01:15.134 --> 00:01:17.344
of it, which is the YL ending.

00:01:17.824 --> 00:01:20.404
Now, being that these
are alkyl chains, right?

00:01:20.744 --> 00:01:24.964
We will always be using that,
that YL ending to denote that,

00:01:24.975 --> 00:01:30.425
hey, we have a branch, or as I
mentioned before, a substituent.

00:01:31.055 --> 00:01:33.545
So both names can be used in this case.

00:01:33.955 --> 00:01:37.934
So methyl is for one carbon, and we
can continue to name the rest of them.

00:01:38.334 --> 00:01:40.595
So two is going off eth, right?

00:01:40.605 --> 00:01:43.915
Think of like ethane, ethanol,
all those different species.

00:01:44.245 --> 00:01:46.155
We're going to keep that beginning prefix.

00:01:46.565 --> 00:01:49.815
And then put the YL ending on it as well.

00:01:49.825 --> 00:01:51.515
So I'm going to put these YLs here.

00:01:52.145 --> 00:01:55.925
So we have ethyl, then we have
three carbons, which is propyl.

00:01:56.404 --> 00:01:58.735
Then we have four carbons, which is butyl.

00:01:59.205 --> 00:02:01.874
And then we have five
carbons, which is pentyl.

00:02:02.345 --> 00:02:05.255
So it's taking that beginning
part of that name, right?

00:02:05.255 --> 00:02:07.995
And this is something, if you're
taking organic chemistry, you need

00:02:08.005 --> 00:02:09.925
to memorize these different endings.

00:02:10.165 --> 00:02:12.585
They are going to be a huge
part when it comes to naming

00:02:12.585 --> 00:02:16.460
structures, drawing structures,
and really diving into all these.

00:02:16.740 --> 00:02:20.820
So let's go through this and do a how
to guide of exactly how to name them.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:30.130
So first things first, when you are
naming these, you have to identify and

00:02:30.130 --> 00:02:32.520
name what the longest carbon chain is.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.100
So you have to be comfortable with
knowing your carbons from generally

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.749
from one to 10 when you're doing these.

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:43.660
Now, when I refer to the parent chain or
the longest carbon chain, This has to be

00:02:43.679 --> 00:02:46.339
continuous carbons connected together.

00:02:46.570 --> 00:02:50.010
So it has to be in one solid line
when you're going through it.

00:02:50.649 --> 00:02:54.249
The second step is we're going
to be identifying all branches.

00:02:54.529 --> 00:02:56.689
There's a number of ways
that you can try to do this.

00:02:56.689 --> 00:03:00.190
You can circle them, you can underline
them, you put arrows on them, star them.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:02.700
But you need to be able
to identify your branches.

00:03:03.230 --> 00:03:07.399
Then you need to number your
carbons, so on your parent chain.

00:03:07.709 --> 00:03:11.359
And you want to start on the
end that is closest to a branch.

00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:13.239
And we're going to go through
examples to show this.

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:17.609
Then you want to give the
location and name each branch in

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:20.199
alphabetical order when we do this.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.820
Sometimes you might have identical
branches as well, and we'll have to

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.830
fold in some special prefixes as well.

00:03:27.220 --> 00:03:30.959
Let's take a look at our first examples
and use these steps to help us.

